---
import Download from '../../components/Download.astro'
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import '../../styles/download.css'
import '../../styles/page.css'
import '../../styles/page-lists.css'
---

<Base
  title="Download Mortar"
  description="Download Mortar for Windows, Linux or the Steam Deck: installer, AppImage, Flatpak, and signed apt, dnf and pacman repositories that keep it up to date."
  current="download"
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Download', href: '/download/' }]}
  band={false}
>
  <header class="wrap pagehead">
    <p class="kicker">Download</p>
    <h1>Get Mortar. <em>It's free.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Free and open source. <span id="version">The latest release</span> for Windows and Linux, on x64 and ARM64, and the Steam Deck.</p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <Download />
  </div>
  <div class="wrap sec dl">
    <div class="head"><p class="kicker">Every way to install</p><h2>Pick your system.</h2></div>
<div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Platform">
  <button
    type="button"
    role="tab"
    id="tab-windows"
    aria-controls="windows"
  ><svg class="mk mk-windows" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#windows" /></svg>Windows</button>
  <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-linux" aria-controls="linux"><svg class="mk mk-linux" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#linux" /></svg>Linux</button>
  <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-deck" aria-controls="deck"><svg class="mk mk-steamdeck" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#steamdeck" /></svg>Steam Deck</button>
  <button
    type="button"
    role="tab"
    id="tab-extension"
    aria-controls="extension"
  ><svg class="mk mk-extension" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#extension" /></svg>Browser extension</button>
</div>

<section
  class="panel"
  id="windows"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="tab-windows"
>
  <p class="muted">
    Windows 10 and 11. Mortar installs for your user, so it needs no
    administrator rights.
  </p>
  <div class="files">
    <a
      class="file primary"
      data-asset="mortar-amd64-installer.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-amd64-installer.exe"
    >
      <strong>Installer, x64</strong><span>Most PCs</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-arm64-installer.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-arm64-installer.exe"
    >
      <strong>Installer, ARM64</strong
      ><span>Snapdragon and other ARM PCs</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-windows-amd64.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-windows-amd64.exe"
    >
      <strong>Portable, x64</strong><span>Runs from any folder</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      data-asset="mortar-windows-arm64.exe"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-windows-arm64.exe"
    >
      <strong>Portable, ARM64</strong><span>Runs from any folder</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps-list">
    <li>
      Run the installer. It puts Mortar in
      <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Mortar</code> and adds Start Menu and
      Desktop shortcuts.
    </li>
    <li>
      The builds are not code-signed, so SmartScreen says “Windows
      protected your PC” the first time. Choose
      <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>Mortar updates itself from then on.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="muted">
    To check the download before running it, see the <a href="/code-signing/">code signing policy</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Scoop</h3>
  <p class="muted">
    From Mortar's own bucket, with every download pinned by its SHA-256. Scoop then handles updates.
  </p>
  <pre><code>scoop bucket add mortar https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/scoop-bucket
scoop install mortar/mortar</code></pre>
</section>

<section
  class="panel"
  id="linux"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="tab-linux"
>
  <p class="muted">
    Install from the package repository and Mortar updates with the rest
    of your system. Pick your distribution:
  </p>
  <div class="tabs sub" role="tablist" aria-label="Distribution">
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-deb" aria-controls="deb"><svg class="mk mk-debian" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#debian" /></svg>Ubuntu and Debian</button>
    <button
      type="button"
      role="tab"
      id="tab-fedora"
      aria-controls="fedora"
    ><svg class="mk mk-fedora" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#fedora" /></svg>Fedora</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-arch" aria-controls="arch"><svg class="mk mk-archlinux" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#archlinux" /></svg>Arch</button>
    <button
      type="button"
      role="tab"
      id="tab-flatpak"
      aria-controls="flatpak"
    ><svg class="mk mk-flatpak" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#flatpak" /></svg>Flatpak</button>
    <button
      type="button"
      role="tab"
      id="tab-appimage"
      aria-controls="appimage"
    ><svg class="mk mk-appimage" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#appimage" /></svg>AppImage</button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-brew" aria-controls="brew"><svg class="mk mk-homebrew" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/img/brands.svg#homebrew" /></svg>Homebrew</button>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="deb"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-deb"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13 or newer, on amd64 and arm64.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo curl -fsSLo /usr/share/keyrings/mortar-archive-keyring.gpg https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar-archive-keyring.gpg
sudo curl -fsSLo /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mortar.list https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar.list
sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install mortar</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      Or install a single
      <a
        data-asset="mortar_VERSION_amd64.deb"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest"
        >.deb</a
      >
      without updates: <code>sudo apt install ./mortar_*_amd64.deb</code>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="fedora"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-fedora"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Fedora 39 or newer, on x86_64 and aarch64. dnf asks once to import
      the key; check its fingerprint against the one below.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo curl -fsSLo /etc/yum.repos.d/mortar.repo https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar.repo
sudo dnf install mortar</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      Or install a single
      <a
        data-asset="mortar-VERSION-1.x86_64.rpm"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest"
        >.rpm</a
      >
      without updates: <code>sudo dnf install ./mortar-*.rpm</code>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="arch"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-arch"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      x86_64 and aarch64. These commands trust the Mortar key, add the
      repository to <code>/etc/pacman.conf</code> and install.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar-archive-keyring.asc | sudo pacman-key --add -
sudo pacman-key --lsign-key D8B1C4C205C5FB3633CB8B9900B75959B637DCB5
curl -fsSL https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/pacman.conf | sudo tee -a /etc/pacman.conf
sudo pacman -Sy mortar</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      Or install a single
      <a
        data-asset="mortar-VERSION-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest"
        >package</a
      >
      without updates: <code>sudo pacman -U mortar-*.pkg.tar.zst</code>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="flatpak"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-flatpak"
  >
    <p class="muted">
      Any distribution with Flatpak, x86_64 or aarch64. It brings its own libraries, so it also runs on older
      systems. This adds Mortar's signed Flatpak repository, so <code>flatpak update</code> keeps it
      current; the GNOME runtime comes from Flathub.
    </p>
    <pre><code>flatpak install --user https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/flatpak/tech.rethunk.Mortar.flatpakref</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      The sandbox only sees the usual game folders. For a game installed elsewhere, run
      <code>flatpak override --user --filesystem=&lt;folder&gt; tech.rethunk.Mortar</code>. A single
      <a data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.flatpak" href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.flatpak">bundle file</a>
      is also on each release, without updates.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div
    class="panel sub"
    id="appimage"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="tab-appimage"
  >
    <h3>AppImage and portable program</h3>
    <p class="muted">
      Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13, Fedora 39 or a newer Linux (glibc 2.38).
      Both update themselves in place.
    </p>
    <div class="files">
      <a
        class="file primary"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
      >
        <strong>AppImage, x86_64</strong
        ><span>Nothing else to install</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-aarch64.AppImage"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-aarch64.AppImage"
      >
        <strong>AppImage, ARM64</strong
        ><span>Nothing else to install</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-amd64"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-amd64"
      >
        <strong>Portable, x86_64</strong
        ><span>Uses the system's GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="file"
        data-asset="mortar-linux-arm64"
        href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-arm64"
      >
        <strong>Portable, ARM64</strong
        ><span>Uses the system's GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <pre><code>chmod +x mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage
./mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
      The first run adds Mortar to your applications menu and takes
      <code>nxm://</code> links and <code>.mortar</code> files.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel sub" id="brew" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-brew">
    <p class="muted">
      From Mortar's own tap, pinned by SHA-256. It installs the AppImage, which needs FUSE 2
      (<code>libfuse2</code>). Homebrew then handles updates.
    </p>
    <pre><code>brew install rethunk-tech/tap/mortar</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="fingerprint">
    Packages are signed by “Mortar packages
    &lt;opensource@rethunk.tech&gt;”, fingerprint
    <code>D8B1 C4C2 05C5 FB36 33CB 8B99 00B7 5959 B637 DCB5</code>.
    <a href="/packages/">Repository details</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="deck" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-deck">
  <p class="muted">
    The Steam Deck runs the x86_64 AppImage. Mortar sets up in Desktop Mode, then each profile can be played from
    Game Mode.
  </p>
  <div class="files">
    <a
      class="file primary"
      data-asset="mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/mortar-linux-x86_64.AppImage"
    >
      <strong>AppImage, x86_64</strong><span>For the Steam Deck</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps-list">
    <li>Switch to Desktop Mode and download the AppImage.</li>
    <li>
      Mark it executable: right-click it, <strong>Properties</strong>, <strong>Permissions</strong>, then
      <strong>Is executable</strong>. Run it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Set up your game, then open a profile's menu and choose <strong>Add to Steam</strong>. The profile appears
      in Game Mode, and playing it there starts the game with that profile's mods.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="muted">
    The Flatpak cannot add itself to Steam, so use the AppImage on the Deck. Mortar updates itself in place.
  </p>
</section>

<section
  class="panel"
  id="extension"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="tab-extension"
>
  <p class="muted">
    Optional. It marks Nexus Mods pages with what your profile already has
    and hands Mod Manager Download clicks to Mortar. Mortar's Settings ›
    Downloads links it too. <a href="/extension/">What it does, and privacy</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="files">
    <a
      class="file primary"
      href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar-browser-extension/releases/latest/download/mortar-browser-extension.xpi"
    >
      <strong>Firefox</strong
      ><span>Signed by Mozilla; open the file in Firefox</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="file"
      href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/hboeppdoecbglcmfojappgehkbecdfbi"
    >
      <strong>Chrome, Edge and other Chromium browsers</strong
      ><span>Chrome Web Store; updates itself</span>
    </a>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps-list">
    <li>
      Chromium browsers: choose <strong>Add to Chrome</strong> on the
      Chrome Web Store page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Firefox: open the <code>.xpi</code> in Firefox, or drop it on
      <code>about:addons</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>
<section class="panel" id="verify">
  <h2>Verify your download</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    Every release carries <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, signed with the Mortar packages key, and a GitHub build
    provenance attestation for each file. With the downloaded file in the current folder:
  </p>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSLO https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/SHA256SUMS
curl -fsSLO https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest/download/SHA256SUMS.asc
curl -fsSL https://mortar.rethunk.tech/packages/mortar-archive-keyring.asc | gpg --import
gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS
sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS
gh attestation verify &lt;file&gt; --repo Rethunk-Tech/mortar</code></pre>
  <p class="muted">The key's fingerprint is <code>D8B1 C4C2 05C5 FB36 33CB 8B99 00B7 5959 B637 DCB5</code>. More in the <a href="/code-signing/">code signing policy</a>.</p>
</section>


<p class="muted all">
  Checksums, release notes and every build:
  <a href="https://github.com/Rethunk-Tech/mortar/releases/latest"
    >latest release on GitHub</a
  >.
</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<script is:inline src="/download.js" defer></script>
